  <bioghist id="4310153a38c2bc3c505c4a1f312ed496">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>Harper Woodward was born in Rochester, New York in 1909. A graduate of Harvard College in 1931 and Harvard Law School 1934, he spent one year as Secretary to President James B. Conant of Harvard, then became a practicing attorney in New York City and Rochester, specializing in aviation matters.</p><p>During World War II, Woodward attained the rank of Lt. Colonel in the United States Air Force. From 1943-1945, he served as a member of the Advisory Council to the General of the Army H. H. Arnold. </p><p>Following the war, Woodward was appointed Director of the Aviation Division, Surplus Property Administration in Washington, DC. He joined Rockefeller Family and Associates in May 1946.</p><p>Woodward was a Director of Eastern Airlines, Itek Corporation, GAB Business Services, Inc., Caneel Bay Plantation, Inc., Coherent Radiation, Mauna Kea Beach Hotel Corporation, Rockresorts, Inc., and the Wings Club. He served as Vice President and member of the Board of Managers of Memorial Hospital; Vice Chairman of the Executive Committee of the Hospital; and a member of the Board of Trustees of Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center and of Sloan-Kettering Institute for Cancer Research.</p><p>Woodward was also a member of the American Yacht Club and the Mansuring Island Club (both in Rye, NY) and the Metropolitan Club (in Washington, D.C.).</p>  </bioghist>
  <scopecontent id="7cd579ab0d0f2712adbd68cdc38e3e2e">
    <head>Scope and Contents</head>
<p>Correspondence, memos and reports primarily pertaining to Eastern Airlines and other aviation interests, Memorial Hospital, Sloan-Kettering Institute and Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center. </p><p>Records also document the institutions and foundations for which Harper Woodward served as a Board or Club member, as well as reports and analysis of various investments made by or on behalf of Laurance S. Rockefeller.</p>  </scopecontent>
